# Untitled

## Functional tuning and expanding of myoglobin by rational protein design
\(^{1}\)School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, University of South China, Hengyang 421001, China \(^{2}\)Laboratory of Noncoding RNA, Institute of Biophysics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100101, China \(^{3}\)Department of Chemistry, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, Urbana, Illinois 61801, USA
Rational protein design is a powerful strategy, not only for revealing the structure and function relationship of natural metalloproteins, but also for creating artificial metalloproteins with improved properties and functions. Myoglobin (Mb), a small heme protein created by nature with diverse functions, has been shown to be an ideal scaffold for rational protein design. The progress reviewed herein includes fine-tuning its native functions of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding and transport, peroxidase activity and nitrite reductase (NIR) activity, and rational expanding its functionalities to peroxygenase, heme-copper oxidase (HCO), nitric oxide reductase (NOR), as well as hydroxylamine reductase. These studies have enhanced our understanding of how metalloproteins work in nature, and provided insights for rational design of functional metalloproteins for practical applications in the future.
metalloproteins, heme proteins, protein design, oxidase, reductase
## 1 Introduction
Metalloproteins account for nearly half of all proteins in nature and play crucial functions in biological systems [1]. Among them, heme proteins consist of a major class, with functions arranging from electron transfer (e.g. cytochrome \(c\), cyt \(c\) and cytochrome \(b_{5}\), cyt \(b_{5}\)), oxygen binding and transport (e.g. myoglobin, Mb and hemoglobin, Hb), catalysis (e.g. cytochrome P450, cyt P450) to signaling (e.g. CO sensor CooA) [2]. It is remarkable that nature uses the same heme group to perform such a large array of functions. This functional diversity is required, as metal ions used by nature are limited to a small subset of physiological available ones [3]. To reveal the structure and function relationships of metalloproteins, including heme proteins, is long-term goal in chemical biology. In addition to studying native metalloproteins, an alternative approach to this goal is to design artificial metalloproteins using a simple protein as a scaffold [4–24]. This "bottom-up" approach can not only reveal the minimal structural element responsible for the function of native metalloproteins, but also address important chemical and biological issues otherwise can hardly be achieved by studying native metalloproteins [1]. Mb is a well-studied representative heme protein, with structure resolved more than half a century ago [25]. Due to the small size (153 amino acids) with a single heme group, the ease of recombinant overexpression and crystallization, Mb has shown to be an ideal scaffold for functional protein design [1, 2, 16–24]. From a functional perspective, rational protein design using Mb as a scaffold can be divided into two major aspects, i.e., functional tuning of Mb based on its native functions and functional expanding of Mb to the function of other heme proteins. In addition to the well-known function of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding and transport, other functions were also discovered for Mb [26–28], such as peroxidase activity and nitrite reductase (NIR) activity. Fine-tuning the native functions of Mb represents an efficient strategy for
artificial metalloproteins using a simple protein as a scaffold [4–24]. This "bottom-up" approach can not only reveal the minimal structural element responsible for the function of native metalloproteins, but also address important chemical and biological issues otherwise can hardly be achieved by studying native metalloproteins [1].
Mb is a well-studied representative heme protein, with structure resolved more than half a century ago [25]. Due to the small size (153 amino acids) with a single heme group, the ease of recombinant overexpression and crystallization, Mb has shown to be an ideal scaffold for functional protein design [1, 2, 16–24]. From a functional perspective, rational protein design using Mb as a scaffold can be divided into two major aspects, i.e., functional tuning of Mb based on its native functions and functional expanding of Mb to the function of other heme proteins. In addition to the wellknown function of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding and transport, other functions were also discovered for Mb [26–28], such as peroxidase activity and nitrite reductase (NIR) activity. Fine-tuning the native functions of Mb represents an efficient strategy for
understanding the delicate structure-function relationship of heme proteins. On the other hand, expanding the functionalities of Mb, such as to the function of heme-copper oxidase (HCO) and nitric oxide reductase (NOR), represents an ability of creating artificial proteins with novel properties and functions, which also ultimately tests of our knowledge of how metalloproteins work in nature. In this review, we focus on the progresses in these two major aspects by summarizing the work from our groups, as well as the contributions from others (Figure 1).
## 2 Tuning the function of Mb by rational protein design
## 2.1 \(\mathbf{O}_{2}\) binding and transport
It is well-known that Mb functions as \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) transporter in vertebrates, in which the heme distal His64 plays a key role in stabilization of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding by a hydrogen-bonding interaction [29]. Meanwhile, the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity of Mb is considerably low with respect to other heme proteins found in microorganisms and plants, such as the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) -avid Hb of Ascaris [30]. The latter exhibits an extremely high \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity with a binding constant of \(3.8\times10^{8}\;\mathrm{M}^{-1}\) , as well as a high \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) selectivity over CO \((M^{\prime}=\textit{K}_{\mathrm{CO}}\,/\,K_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}\ =0.02–0.03)\) , which are
attributed to a special hydrogen-bonding network in the distal site. To fine-tune the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity of Mb, Brunori and co-workers [31] simultaneously replaced residues at positions 29, 64 and 67 by Tyr, His and Arg, respectively, to mimic the active site in Hb of Ascaris. It was shown that the introduction of both \(\mathrm{Tyr}29\) and His64 is associated with a large decrease in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) dissociation rate constant, suggesting a possible formation of two hydrogen bonds with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) upon its binding.
Although construction of a distal hydrogen-bonding network is straightforward for fine-tuning the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity of Mb, no satisfactory results have been obtained yet. Alternat
tively, replacement of the native heme group with an artificial metal complex of a similar structure has been shown to be an efficient way to tune the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity of Mb. As shown by Neya et al. [32], the deoxy forms of two Mbs, reconstituted with \(\upalpha\) -azamesophorphrin XIII and \(\upbeta,\updelta\) -diazamesophorphrin III, exhibit enhanced \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinities with binding constants of \(5.5\times10^{7}\) and \(3.05\times10^{7}\;\mathrm{M}^{-1}\) , respectively, which are about 40–100-fold higher than that of native Mb. Moreover, Hayashi and co-workers [33–35] showed that reconstitution of apoMb with iron porphycene carrying two propionate groups, the artificial protein (Figure 2) exhibits a dramatically enhanced \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding affinity \((1.6\times10^{9}\;\mathrm{M}^{-1})\) , which is about 2300-fold higher than that of native Mb. At the same time, the CO affinity for this artificial protein is not remarkably enhanced, which results in a \(M^{\prime}\) value \((\,K_{\mathrm{CO}}\,/\,K_{\mathrm{O}_{2}}\,)\) of 0.1, as compared to a value of 16 for wildtype (WT) Mb, indicating a dramatically change for the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}\) discrimination. In addition, cobaltous porphycene was found to be capable of stabilizing \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in Mb by 100-fold compared to that of Mb reconstituted with the protoporphyrin cobalt complex [36]. These studies thus demonstrate that the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) affinity of Mb can be rationally tuned by the replacement of the native heme with an appropriate metal complex, and valuable information can be obtained for understanding ligand-metal-protein interactions.
## 2.2 Peroxidase activity
In addition to being an \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) carrier, Mb was also found to act as a peroxidase and scavenge \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) [37–39], a major reactive oxygen species (ROS) produced endogenously when tissues are reoxygenated after hypoxia, thereby protecting against oxidative damage [38, 40]. Meanwhile the peroxidase activity of Mb is quite lower compared to native peroxidases, by lacking an appropriate \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation system and a substrate-binding site near the heme pocket. The heme active site shows that the distal His64 (Nε atom) is located \(4.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\) away from the heme iron center in sperm whale Mb (Figure 3(a)) [41], whereas the distance is \(5.5–6.0\mathrm{~\AA~}\) for
distal histidine in native peroxidases, such as horseradish peroxidase (HRP) [42] and cytochrome \(c\) peroxidase (CcP) [43]. Based on this difference, Watanabe and co-workers [44] constructed a series of Mb mutants, which contains no distal histidine (H64L Mb), or distal histidine with different locations, F43H/H64L Mb (H43-Ne atom-heme iron distance \(5.7\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ), and L29H/H64L Mb (H29-Ne atom-heme iron distance \(6.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ) (Figures 3(b) and (c)). It was found that the peroxidase activity of H64L Mb and H64L/L29H Mb decreased about 100-fold and 3–6-fold, respectively, whereas H64L/F43H Mb increased about 11-fold compared to WT Mb [45]. These observations indicate that proper positioning of a distal histidine with a suitable distance to the heme iron is essential for the activation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\).
Recently, Lin and co-workers [46] found that by retaining the native His64, the second His29 in the L29H Mb mutant works cooperatively with the native His64 in oxidizing guaiacol, resulting in about 3.7-fold increase of peroxidase activity. Moreover, L29H Mb shows a similar enhanced peroxidase-like activity in didecyldimethylammonium bromide (DDAB) film on glassy carbon electrode (GCE) by using \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), instead of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), as an oxidant [47]. Interestingly, it was also found that introduction of a third distal histidine in the heme pocket, L29H/F43H Mb, can not further increase the peroxidase activity of Mb [48]. At the same time, since His29 and His43, together with the native His64, form a metal-binding site as demonstrated previously [49], it was shown that the binding of metal ions such \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) and \(\mathrm{Zn(II)}\) inhibits the catalytic activity for a different extent, i.e. about \(64\%\) and \(40\%\), respectively, likely by blocking the role of the distal histidines as an acid-base catalyst (Figure 4). These observations suggest that the peroxidase activity of Mb can be fine-tuned by introducing distal histidines as well as by metal-binding in the heme distal pocket.
In case of tuning the peroxidase of Mb by metal ions, Hunter et al. [50] provided another example by creating a metal-binding site on protein surface stead of in the heme pocket. They found that replacement of two surface residues, Lys45 and Lys63, with Glu results in a mutant of K45E/ K63E Mb that readily binds \(\mathrm{Mn(II)}\). The crystal structure of this mutant (Figure 5) shows that Glu45, His113, two water molecules and the heme 6-propinate group coordinate the
\(\mathrm{Mn(II)}\) ion, forming a six-coordination structure, which closely mimics the active site of native manganese peroxidase (MnP). More importantly, K45E/K63E Mb exhibits about 3-fold increase in MnP activity relative to WT Mb. On the other hand, Zhang and co-workers [51] used \(\mathrm{Mn(II)}\) protoporphyrin as an artificial prosthetic group to tune the peroxidase activity of Mb, and observed a cooperative effect of dual distal histidines at positions 43 and 64 in F43H \(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Mb}\) on the activation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), with a 5-fold reaction rate increase versus WT \(\mathrm{Mn}^{\mathrm{III}}\mathrm{Mb}\).
Different from above studies, Dawson and co-workers [52] recently designed two peroxidase-like Mb mutants, G65T Mb and G65I Mb, by introducing a larger side chain at position 65 between helixes B and E, which pushes His64 a little far from the heme iron center and results in a distance of 4.6 and \(5.7\,\mathrm{\AA}\), respectively. It was shown that G65T Mb and G65I Mb has about 3.8-fold and 46-fold
increased peroxidase activity compared to WT Mb, respectively, which indicates that the peroxidase activity of Mb can also be fine-tuned indirectly by steric hindrance effect instead of direct modification of distal histidine or heme group [17].
## 2.3 Nitrite reductase (NIR) activity
In recent years, another important function of NIR activity was discovered for Mb [27, 53], which showed that Mb regulates mitochondrial function through its NIR activity by reducing nitrite \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\right)\)to NO under hypoxic conditions, i.e., \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}+\) deoxy \(\mathrm{Mb}(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})+\mathrm{H}^{+}\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}+\mathrm{metMb}(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+})+\mathrm{OH}^{-}\). To probe this novel structure-function relationship of Mb, Richter-Addo and co-workers [54, 55] carried out some initial studies, and found that the distal His64 in heme pocket directs an O-binding model of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\)binding to the heme iron and plays a crucial role in nitrite reduction. It was shown that the NIR activity of H64V Mb, which lacks a critical hydrogen-bonding residue, is only about 1/16 that of WT Mb. The activity of H64V Mb can be increased 5-fold by introducing an Arg residue at position 67, i.e., H64V/V67R Mb, where Arg67 forms a hydrogen-bonding with nitrite upon its binding [54]. These observations suggest that the presence of a distal residue capable of forming a hydrogen-bonding is critical for the NIR activity. On the other hand, it was also found that the NIR activity of Mb can be tuned by substitution of heme Fe(II) with \(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})\) or \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\), resulting in much less reactive than WT Mb in nitrite reduction [56].
To further reveal the structure and NIR function relationship of Mb, Lin and co-workers [57] interested in whether multiple distal histidine could fine-tune the reactivity, and made both a structural and NIR activity comparisons of Mb with one to three distal histidines, i.e., WT Mb, L29H Mb and L29H/F43H Mb (Figure 6). It was shown that introduction of one or two additional distal histidines in Mb leads to the formation of a distal hydrogen network, which slightly alters the conformation of heme active site and inhibits the NIR activity. This observation in turn suggests that a single distal histidine is enough for providing the proton required for nitrite reduction and multiple distal histidines can not further improve the NIR activity of Mb. Meanwhile, it was found that the NIR activity of both L29H Mb and L29H/F43H Mb can be regulated by metal ions such as \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) or \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\) binding to the distal histidines, i.e., \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) enhances the reactivity while \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\) inhibits the reactivity. The copper ion in the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\) site likely facilitates the binding of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\)and/or the cleavage of N–O bond, whereas the binding of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\) does not, instead, it likely blocks the essential role such as providing proton of the distal histidines in NIR activity. It is interesting to note that \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\)-L29H/F43H Mb closely mimics native cyt \(c\) oxidase \((\mathrm{CcO})\) with a \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\)-heme binuclear metal center, and the latter was also found to display a novel NIR activity [58]. These
findings thus provide valuable insights into the structure and function relationship for both Mb and \(\mathrm{CcO}\) functioning as a NIR in biological system.
Historically, biological reduction of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\)to NO has primarily associated with denitrifying bacteria, as catalyzed by cyt \(cd_{1}\) NIR from \(Ps.\) aeruginosa that contains two distal histidines and one distal tyrosine in the active heme \(d_{1}\) center [59]. To mimic the active site of cyt \(cd_{1}\) NIR and to find whether it could fine-tune the NIR activity of Mb, Lin et al. [60] designed a L29H/F43Y Mb mutant, in which an additional distal histidine was introduced at position 29 and the distal Phe43 was mutated to a Tyr. The modeling structure of L29H/F43Y Mb shows that distal His29, His64 and Tyr43 are in good position with respect to that in cyt \(cd_{1}\) NIR (Figure 7(a)), and thus could provide comparable interactions with substrate nitrite as in native enzymes. The structure of L29H/F43Y Mb was further confirmed by an X-ray structure resolved recently (Figure 7(b)). Moreover, we found that L29H/F43Y Mb and single mutants such as F43Y Mb and F43H Mb, exhibit an enhanced NIR activity compared to WT Mb, by transferring an oxygen atom of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\)to substrate such as cysteine, resulting in formation of \(\mathrm{NO-Mb}(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+})\) and the corresponding sulfenic acid (to be published). Other factors such as heme type (heme \(b\) vs. heme \(d_{1}\)) may also regulate the NIR activity, which are currently under investigations.
## 3 Expanding the function of Mb by rational protein design
## 3.1 Peroxygenase activity
Although Mb exhibits peroxidase activity, both peroxidases and Mb are not good enzymes for cya P450-type monooxygenation [61], which directs efforts to the transformation of peroxidases such as HRP [62] into peroxygenase, i.e., peroxide dependent monooxygenase, including efforts for Mb. For example, Levinger et al. [63] found that the peroxygenase activity of Mb is enhanced about 25-fold in oxidation of styrene by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) with a single active site mutation of Phe43 to Tyr43. On the other hand, Hara et al. [64] showed that with Phe43 replaced by Trp43, the F43W Mb mutant is capable of oxidizing the aromatic ring of Trp43 upon addition of an oxidant, \(m\)-chloroperbenzoic acid.
In an attempt to expand the peroxygenase activity of Mb, Watanabe and co-workers [65, 66] reported a method for reconstituting apoMb with metal-Schiff bases such as Fe/ Mn/Cr-salophen/salen. Due to the structural similarity between salophen/salen and heme, metal complexes, such as Mn-salophen, can bind tightly in the heme pocket with the same coordination geometry as heme (Figure 8(a)). Moreover, the enantioselectivity of sulfoxidation can be controlled by changing the size of substituents at the 3 and \(3^{\prime}\) positions of salen incorporated into apo-A71G Mb [67]. More recently, Hayashi and co-workers [68] created a new biocatalyst by reconstituting Mb with manganese porphycene, a structural isomer of heme, which catalyzes the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-dependent hydroxylation of ethylbenzene to yield 1-phenyle thanol as a single product with a turnover number of 13 at \(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(\mathrm{pH}\,8.5\). Note that native Mb and other modified Mbs do not catalyze \(\mathrm{C-H}\) hydroxylation of alkanes.
In above studies, the artificial cofactor was associated with the protein matrix non-covalently. To overcome the disadvantage such as loss of the cofactor and potentially to increase its catalytic efficiency, Lu and co-workers [69] developed an alternative approach by attaching a Mn-salen complex to apo-Tyr103Cys Mb through a single Cys-selective linker, which results in about \(12\%\) enantiomeric excess towards sulfoxidation of thioanisole, compared to about \(0\%\) for non-covalent incorporation. Furthermore, addition of a second linker (Figure 8(b)) to Mn-salen leads to a significantly increases enantiomeric selectivity (about \(51\%\) enantiomeric excess), suggesting the dual anchoring approach is the most effective in restricting the conformational freedom of the artificial enzyme, resulting in higher enantioselectivity. Moreover, the positions of the anchors were found to play an important role in tuning the reactivity and selectivity of sulfoxidation. For example, changing the left anchor from Y103C to T39C leads to a 1.8-fold increase in rate and a \(14\%\) increase in selectivity for S, regardless of whether the right anchor was L72C or S108C. At the same time, regardless of the identity of the left anchor, changing the right anchor from S108C to L72C yields a 4-fold increase in rate and a \(66\%\) increase in selectivity [70]. These studies demonstrate that the functionality of Mb can be successfully expanded to peroxygenase activity, which is also tunable by rational protein design.
