## 3.2 Heme-copper oxidase (HCO) activity
HCOs are large membrane-bound proteins that catalyze proton-coupled reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to water. It contains a copper \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\right)\) above the heme group, forming a unique \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\)-heme dinuclear center [71]. While Mb is one of the most well-characterized heme proteins, the structure-function relationship of HCOs remains to be fully understood. To address important issues such as the role of the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\) center, Lu and co-workers [49] explored a novel strategy by design and engineering of a \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\) center in Mb to expand the functionality of Mb to HCO. Based on structural comparison and computer modeling of Mb and HCO, two distal histidines were rationally introduced into the heme pocket of sperm whale Mb by mutation of Leu29 and Phe43. As confirmed by a series of spectroscopic studies, the resultant His29, His43, together with the native His64, formed a 3-His copper-binding site \(\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\right.\) site) with a \(K_{\mathrm{D}}\) of \(9~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) in the variant protein, called \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\). Further studies [49, 72] of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\) showed that \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})\) plays a critical role in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) binding and reduction, and in modulating the redox potential of the heme when it is coupled with copper. Moreover, it revealed that both heme type [73] and the presence of chloride [74, 75] play a role in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction by \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\).
However, instead of reducing \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to water as expected, the copper-loaded \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\), \(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{II})-\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\), functioned as a copper-dependent heme oxygenase, degrading the heme to verdroheme [72]. Lu and co-workers [76] hypothesized that the lack of a tyrosine next to one of the distal histidines in \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\), which is a feature of native HCOs, may limit the conversion of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to water. To address this problem, they introduced Tyr residues at two different positions (33 and 65) close to the histidines of the \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\) site (Figure 9). The resulting enzymes were found to efficiently reduce \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to water (G65Y \(\mathrm{Cu}_{\mathrm{B}}\mathrm{Mb}\) with over 1000 turnovers) with minimal
release of superoxide or peroxide.
Moreover, the crystal structure of HCO reveals a novel Tyr-His cross-link between carbon atom C6 of the Tyr and nitrogen atom \(\mathrm{N}\varepsilon\) of a nearby His in the heme active site [77]. To reveal the biological role of this cross-link, Wang, Lu and co-workers [78] incorporated an unnatural amino acid imiTyr, which mimics the cross-linked Try-His ligand, into a functional protein model of HCO based on Mb, called imiTyr \(\mathrm{Cu_{B}Mb}\) (Figure 10). The rationally designed HCO model of imiTyr \(\mathrm{Cu_{B}Mb}\) was found to catalyze the reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to water, with 8-fold more selectivity and 3-fold more turnovers than its natural counterpart F33Y \(\mathrm{Cu_{B}Mb}\), which does not contain the cross-link but harbors His and Tyr residues at the same position in the same protein scaffold. This study thereby demonstrates that the Tyr-His cross-link is indeed essential for HCOs function. These findings further illustrate that functional expanding of Mb to HCO by rational design can reveal the hidden structural features responsible for the protein function, which otherwise are difficult to obtain by studying native HCOs.
## 3.3 Nitric oxide reductase (NOR) activity
NOR is one of metalloenzymes in denitrification pathway responsible for the two-electron reduction of NO to \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)
To reveal the roles of both metal ion and conserved glutamates in \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}}\) site for native NOR, Lu and co-workers [83] introduced one glutamate by V68E mutation into the heme pocket of \(\mathrm{Cu_{B}Mb}\), named \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}Mb}\). Computer modeling and dynamic simulation indicated that the 3-His-1-Glu metal-binding site could support the formation of an \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}}\) site as in native NOR. As shown in Figure 11(a), the in silico designed protein model was then confirmed by a high resolution (1.72 Å) crystal structure of \(\mathrm{Fe(II)-Fe_{B}Mb}\), with both structures overlapped well with each other. Moreover, it was found that introduction of a second Glu in the secondary sphere of \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}}\) site (Ile107Glu \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}Mb}\)) results in about 100% increase in NOR activity, suggesting the importance of a hydrogen bonding network in facilitating proton delivery during NOR reactivity [84].
One year after the design of \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}Mb}\), Shiro and coworkers [80] reported the crystal structure of a bacterial NOR from Pseudomonas aeruginosa at 2.7 Å resolution. This structure reveals that \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}}\) is coordinated by three His and one Glu ligands, which thus validates the designed protein model of \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}Mb}\). More excitingly, the active site of \(\mathrm{Fe_{B}Mb}\) overlays closely with that of the native NOR, not only the ligands, but also the heme-non-heme iron center (Figure 11(b)).
Lu and co-workers [85] further showed that a conserved 2-His-1-Glu metal-binding center, as found in natural non-heme iron-containing enzymes [86], can also be engineered in Mb by double mutations (L29E/F43H Mb), and confers
NOR activities with \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{II})\) or \(\mathrm{Cu(I)}\) binding to the non-heme binding site. This functional expanding study of Mb thus presents a novel structural and functional protein model for NOR. At the same time, this study revealed that not only protein function but also protein folding are controlled by metal ions. For example, the crystal structure of \(\mathrm{Cu(II)-}\) \(\mathrm{CN^{-}-L29E/F43H}\) Mb shows that the protein adopts a native fold with \(\mathrm{Cu(II)}\) bound and bridged by a cyanide (Figure 12(a)). Meanwhile, the mutant was isolated as a low-spin species with bis-His coordination in the absence of metal bound, as indicated by UV-Vis and EPR studies [85]. To predict the structure of L29E/F43H Mb in this unique non-native state, Lin [87] performed a molecular modeling study. It revealed that due to the flexibility of the helixes CDE helices of Mb, introduction of a hydrophilic group by mutation of Leu29 to \(\mathrm{Glu}29\) in the hydrophobic heme pocket alters the protein folding behavior of Mb, resulting in the formation of bis-His coordination (His64/His93). Glu29 was also found to interact with His64 by hydrogen bonding interactions and stabilize this unique non-native state (Figure 12(b)).
## 3.4 Hydroxylamine reductase activity
The functional significance of certain post-translational modifications in natural proteins, such as the Tyr-His crosslink in HCOs [77], is difficult to probe by traditional site-directed mutagenesis approaches, which however can be well-addressed by introduction of unnatural amino acid [78]. Another cross-link of Tyr-Cys is ubiquitous for diverse metalloenzymes, including galactose oxidase (GO) [88], cysteine dioxygenase (CDO) [89], sulfite reductase (NirA) [90], and \(T.\) nitratireducens cyt \(c\) nitrite reductase (TvNiR) [91]. To probe the functional significance of the essential Tyr-Cys cofactor in native enzymes, Wang and co-workers [92] genetically incorporated an unnatural amino acid of MtTyr into Mb, called MtTyrMb (Figure 13), which mimics the Tyr-Cys cofactor and expands Mb to a functional model of of TvNiR. It was found that MtTyrMb exhibits a novel hydroxylamine reductase and catalyzes efficiently and selectively hydroxylamine to ammonia, which is 4-fold more active than TyrMb with no 3-methylthio substitution at residue Tyr33. These results provide the first direct evidence
that a 3-methylthio substitution on a tyrosine residue can enhance enzyme activity. The designed enzyme serves as an ideal model for mechanic study of native metalloenzymes and allows for potential applications in synthetic biology and industrial catalysis.
## 4 Summary and perspectives
In summary, nature created a small heme protein, Mb, with diverse functions, which has been shown to be an ideal scaffold for functional protein design, and enables us to reveal the secrets of metalloproteins in nature. On one hand, functional tuning of Mb provides valuable information for understanding the precise structure and function relationship of metalloproteins. On the other hand, functional expanding of Mb endows us the ability to create new enzymes with improved properties and functions. Although we focus only on Mb in this review, nature created other wonderful proteins such as cyt \(c\) [93–95], azurin [96–101], and green fluorescent protein (GFP) [102–104], which are also suitable for rational protein design. During this process, various approaches have been developed, such as design of non-heme metal-binding sites [1, 2, 13–15, 18–24], introduction of non-native metal-containing cofactors [1, 2, 12, 15–24], and introduction of unnatural amino acids [1, 2, 18–20, 23, 105]. Moreover, new approaches are continuously being developed. For example, Wang and co-workers [103, 104] recently reported genetic incorporation of a few metal-chelating unnatural amino acids, tyrosine derivatives with altered \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) and reduction potentials [106], and unnatural amino acids with photoclick reactivity [107–109], for expanding the functionalities of natural proteins. In addition to redesign of natural proteins, de novo protein design represents another powerful strategy for creating artificial metalloproteins with novel functions, which promises both in
vitro and in vivo applications in the future [1, 12–14, 110–112]. It is reasonable to believe that we will all be benefit from the functional metalloprotein design, which is limited only by our thoughts.
